A||||5||fieroX||question about seats||||1||
Z||000000||fieroX||12-03-2001||02:08 AM||||Rare87GT purchased a 86 2m6 last week, and the passenger seat is in pretty good shape, as are most passenger seats.  What I was wondering is, next spring I will be purchasing a set of Mr. Mikes, but I dont want the hassle of fixing my severly torn foam padding on my existing driver seat, so Is it possible to take the seat frame that is in the car now, and just put the padding from the 2m6 passenger seat on it?  I guess that sounds kind of stupid, because I know it can be done, but how difficult is this to do?  What would be the most efficient way to take care of this problem?   Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jefrysuko||12-03-2001||02:14 AM||||Just use the entire passenger seat and put the hardware from the drivers seat on it.  When you take the seat covers off look and see where the mounting holes are and cut out the foam in those places and you will find the threads to mount the hardware.  You can put the pieces of foam in the passenger side holes if you want but the covers will hide them.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroX||12-03-2001||02:19 AM||||I figured it would go something like that.  Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||12-03-2001||06:55 AM||||Only the bottom part of the passenger seat is interchangable with the driver side, the backrest part is not. The foam padding is molded around the seat frame and is not removable||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cozmo||12-03-2001||07:50 AM||||Phil is right, the bottom is interchangeable.<BR>You have to peel back both corners where the back hinges to move the nut that holds the non-latch side to the other side.  You also need to cut 2 new holes in the material for the backrest latch to mount (don't worry, the old holes are hidden by the gas tank hump).<BR>You also have to swap the seat tracks.<BR>The back is more complicated, you need to re-use the original driver's side frame and padding. Unzip the cover (careful!) pull out the 2 metal rods and remove the hog rings. Take the cover off like a sock (inside out) removing hog rings as you go (I re-used mine later).  Slip the new cover over the original driver's side seat back and attach it in the original locations with hog rings then insert the metal rods (this was the hardest part, getting those to line up right).  Zip it up, reassemble and install.  I've done this a few times, it's a cheep and (somewhat) easy way to get nice seats if you can find a donor with the same material and style.<BR>Good luck!    <P>------------------<BR><B>Bob Cardone</B><BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/thankssig.jpg">   <BR><B>'85 2M6 SE Targa</B>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroBUZZ||12-03-2001||01:26 PM||||THe backrests can be removed from the hardware and interchanged as well.  Just a little more work. <BR>Gary||||reg||1
